---
import MarketingPage from '../layouts/MarketingPage.astro';

const articleSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'TechArticle',
  headline: 'Why I Built Shiba: Approval-Gated AI Coding on Your Own Cloudflare',
  description:
    'The story behind Shiba: choose a supported agent harness and model per run, execute inside your own Cloudflare account, and inspect every diff and failure before merge.',
  author: {
    '@type': 'Person',
    name: 'Prince Pal',
    sameAs: ['https://github.com/princepal9120'],
  },
  publisher: {
    '@type': 'Organization',
    name: 'Shiba',
    logo: {
      '@type': 'ImageObject',
      url: 'https://tryshiba.dev/assets/mascot/shiba-logo-512.png',
    },
  },
  datePublished: '2026-09-26',
  dateModified: '2026-10-30',
  mainEntityOfPage: 'https://tryshiba.dev/why-shiba/',
  image: 'https://tryshiba.dev/assets/og/shiba-og.png',
};
---

<MarketingPage
  title="Why I Built Shiba — Approval-Gated AI Coding on Your Own Cloudflare"
  description="The story behind Shiba: choose a supported agent harness and model per run, execute inside your own Cloudflare account, and inspect every diff and failure before merge."
  path="/why-shiba/"
  type="article"
  publishedTime="2026-09-26T00:00:00Z"
  modifiedTime="2026-10-30T00:00:00Z"
  schema={articleSchema}
  breadcrumbs={[
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'Why Shiba', path: '/why-shiba/' },
  ]}
>
  <!-- HERO MANIFESTO -->
  <section class="shell hero">
    <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-3 py-1.5 bg-[var(--card)] border border-[var(--line-2)] text-[10px] font-mono uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-[var(--navy)] mb-6 shadow-[2px_2px_0_var(--paper-shadow)]">
      <span class="w-2 h-2 bg-[var(--navy)] animate-pulse" aria-hidden="true"></span>
      <span>FOUNDER NOTES · OCTOBER 2026</span>
    </div>
    <h1 class="manifesto-title text-[var(--ink)]">
      Devin showed what autonomous coding could be. I wanted to <span class="text-[var(--navy)]">pick the model</span>.
    </h1>
    <p class="lead text-[var(--ink-2)]">
      Shiba is an open-source, approval-gated AI coding coworker deployed inside <em>your own Cloudflare account</em>.
      Select the harness and model per task, require human approval before any sandbox starts, and inspect real diffs and exit codes — including when something fails.
    </p>
    <div class="hero-actions">
      <a class="button" href="/waitlist/">
        <span>Join the Waitlist &amp; Contribute</span>
        <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      </a>
      <a class="button button-outline" href="/open-source-devin-alternative/">
        <span>Read the Evaluation Guide</span>
      </a>
      <a class="button button-outline" href="/docs/getting-started/">
        <span>Quickstart Guide</span>
      </a>
      <a class="button button-outline" href="https://github.com/princepal9120/shiba" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
        <span>GitHub Source ↗</span>
      </a>
    </div>
    <p class="hero-status font-mono text-[11px] text-[var(--ink-2)] mt-6">
      Honest status: a working prototype. Typecheck, lint, imports, tests, and build pass locally. No cloud end-to-end run is recorded; see the remaining verification gaps in <a class="text-[var(--navy)] underline" href="/docs/readiness/">Readiness</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="shell">
    <!-- 00: THE SETUP -->
    <section class="story-section" aria-labelledby="origin-title">
      <div class="section-label"><span class="micro">00 / The Setup</span></div>
      <div class="prose-body">
        <h2 id="origin-title" class="text-[var(--ink)]">Two subscriptions, one workflow, and zero say in model routing</h2>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
          For months I ran Devin alongside my own local CLI setup. Devin's underlying engineering is solid: it handles medium-to-large backend changes without hand-holding, and its sandbox environment removes local machine friction.
        </p>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
          The friction was never coding quality. It was autonomy without choice. A small frontend polish task was routed to the same heavyweight model as a multi-file architecture refactor. When I wanted to use a specialized frontend model, or point a fast model at a routine rename, there was no switch to flip.
        </p>
        <blockquote class="pull-quote">
          I wanted Devin-level background execution and computer use — inside my own harness, on my own routing, deployed in my own cloud account.
        </blockquote>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
          That single requirement is why Shiba exists.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 01: THREE REAL FRICTIONS -->
    <section class="story-section" aria-labelledby="section-friction">
      <div class="section-label"><span class="micro">01 / What Kept Breaking</span></div>
      <div class="prose-body">
        <h2 id="section-friction" class="text-[var(--ink)]">Three friction points in daily agent use</h2>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
          Everything that slowed down daily work came down to ownership, credential exposure, and visibility:
        </p>

        <div class="friction-list my-8">
          <div class="friction-row">
            <div class="friction-num" aria-hidden="true">01</div>
            <div>
              <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-[var(--ink)] mb-2">Fixed vendor model routing</h3>
              <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)] text-sm leading-relaxed mb-0">
                A large refactor needs deep reasoning; a small CSS alignment or documentation tweak needs speed and low token cost. Forcing every prompt through one fixed vendor pipeline wastes both time and budget.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="friction-row">
            <div class="friction-num" aria-hidden="true">02</div>
            <div>
              <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-[var(--ink)] mb-2">Passing real credentials into containers</h3>
              <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)] text-sm leading-relaxed mb-0">
                When a coding agent executes arbitrary code or pulls third-party packages, putting personal API keys or broad GitHub tokens into that environment is dangerous. Real tokens must stay outside the container boundary.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="friction-row">
            <div class="friction-num" aria-hidden="true">03</div>
            <div>
              <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-[var(--ink)] mb-2">Opaque status instead of verifiable evidence</h3>
              <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)] text-sm leading-relaxed mb-0">
                When a background job fails, a developer needs concrete evidence: which files were modified, the exact shell exit code, stderr output, and a unified diff. A vague completed card is not proof of working code.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 02: THE PRODUCT CONTRACT -->
    <section class="story-section" aria-labelledby="section-ask">
      <div class="section-label"><span class="micro">02 / The Ask</span></div>
      <div class="prose-body">
        <h2 id="section-ask" class="text-[var(--ink)]">The Shiba product contract</h2>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
          Shiba is not a clone of any commercial product. It is a self-hosted architecture built on six specific rules:
        </p>
        <ul class="check-list my-8">
          <li><strong>Autonomous execution off your laptop</strong> — tasks execute inside isolated Cloudflare Sandbox containers so your laptop stays quiet and cool.</li>
          <li><strong>Pluggable agent harnesses</strong> — pin and run OpenCode, Claude Code, Codex, or Devin depending on task requirements.</li>
          <li><strong>Granular model routing</strong> — route each run to the best model through Cloudflare AI Gateway BYOK credentials.</li>
          <li><strong>Account-owned infrastructure</strong> — you own the Cloudflare Worker, the D1/Durable Objects storage, and the container runtime.</li>
          <li><strong>Worker-side credentials for supported harnesses</strong> — provider-backed harnesses use dummy keys and Worker-side egress authorization. Antigravity OAuth tokens currently live in a container profile; do not enable it for untrusted repositories. See <a href="/docs/configuration/">configuration limitations</a>.</li>
          <li><strong>Approval gate before execution</strong> — runs require a structured approval card by default before a sandbox starts. Narrow, explicitly enabled unattended automations are an exception; planning can call models before approval.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
          The name comes from the Japanese dog breed — loyal, vigilant, and independent. Shiba runs in your own account and asks for approval before sandbox execution by default, with a narrow opt-in exception for unattended automations.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 03: ROUTING TABLE -->
    <section class="story-section" aria-labelledby="section-routing">
      <div class="section-label"><span class="micro">03 / Routing</span></div>
      <div class="prose-body">
        <h2 id="section-routing" class="text-[var(--ink)]">Routing the right model to the right task</h2>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
          An autonomous agent locked to a single model is inflexible. The Shiba container image packages four pinned CLI harnesses. For every task, you choose the harness and model pairing:
        </p>
        <div class="route-table my-8" role="table" aria-label="Harness and model routing by task type">
          <div class="route-head" role="row">
            <span role="columnheader">Task category</span>
            <span role="columnheader">Recommended route</span>
            <span role="columnheader">Engineering rationale</span>
          </div>
          <div class="route-row" role="row">
            <span role="cell" class="route-task">Frontend &amp; UI</span>
            <span role="cell" class="font-mono text-xs text-[var(--ink)]">UI-focused vision/coding model</span>
            <span role="cell">Fast iterations on styling, layout, and component markup without heavy reasoning overhead.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="route-row" role="row">
            <span role="cell" class="route-task">Architecture &amp; Refactors</span>
            <span role="cell" class="font-mono text-xs text-[var(--ink)]">Deep reasoning model (Opus / Sonnet)</span>
            <span role="cell">Cross-file consistency, type-safety boundaries, and architectural migrations.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="route-row" role="row">
            <span role="cell" class="route-task">Bugfixes &amp; Renames</span>
            <span role="cell" class="font-mono text-xs text-[var(--ink)]">Fast flash model (Gemini 2.5 Flash)</span>
            <span role="cell">High speed, sub-dollar cost per hundred runs, sufficient for bounded fixes.</span>
          </div>
          <div class="route-row" role="row">
            <span role="cell" class="route-task">Long Background Tasks</span>
            <span role="cell" class="font-mono text-xs text-[var(--ink)]">Autonomous container runner</span>
            <span role="cell">Runs test suites, captures diffs, and streams logs back to your dashboard or Slack thread.</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
          Routing resolution follows a deterministic cascade: <strong>per-run prompt override → automation schedule setting → repository purpose policy → deployment default</strong>. Invalid routes fail closed before the approval card is minted.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>

  <div class="shell">
    <!-- 04: THE LANDSCAPE -->
    <section class="story-section" aria-labelledby="section-problem">
      <div class="section-label">
        <span class="micro">04 / The Landscape</span>
      </div>
      <div class="prose-body">
        <h2 id="section-problem" class="text-[var(--ink)]">Three existing trade-offs in AI coding</h2>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
          Developers evaluating coding agents typically balance three distinct trade-offs:
        </p>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-6 my-8">
          <div class="feature-card">
            <div class="card-num" aria-hidden="true">01</div>
            <h3 class="text-[var(--ink)] font-bold text-base mb-2">Hosted SaaS agents</h3>
            <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)] text-xs leading-relaxed">
              Convenient zero-setup infrastructure, but proprietary code, vendor-controlled model routing, and code stored on third-party servers.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="feature-card">
            <div class="card-num" aria-hidden="true">02</div>
            <h3 class="text-[var(--ink)] font-bold text-base mb-2">Local CLI tools</h3>
            <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)] text-xs leading-relaxed">
              Direct machine access and high privacy, but tasks block your local terminal, drain battery, halt when the laptop sleeps, and lack team webhook triggers.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="feature-card">
            <div class="card-num" aria-hidden="true">03</div>
            <h3 class="text-[var(--ink)] font-bold text-base mb-2">Heavy self-hosted stacks</h3>
            <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)] text-xs leading-relaxed">
              Full control, but require operating Kubernetes clusters, Postgres, Redis, Celery, and custom ingress proxies just to run a coding agent.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
          Shiba targets the middle path: <strong>a serverless, single-tenant deployment in your own Cloudflare account</strong> that provisions ephemeral Linux containers on demand.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 05: THE CLOUDFLARE FOUNDATION -->
    <section class="story-section" aria-labelledby="section-cloudflare">
      <div class="section-label">
        <span class="micro">05 / Architecture</span>
      </div>
      <div class="prose-body">
        <h2 id="section-cloudflare" class="text-[var(--ink)]">Why Shiba runs on Cloudflare Workers + Sandbox</h2>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
          Instead of operating long-running virtual machines, Shiba leverages Cloudflare's serverless primitives:
        </p>

        <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
          <div class="pillar-row">
            <div class="pillar-icon" aria-hidden="true">01</div>
            <div>
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-[var(--ink)] mb-1">Cloudflare Workers: Orchestration Brain</h3>
              <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)] text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The planning agent runs as a TypeScript Worker powered by the Cloudflare Agents SDK. It handles webhooks, cron automations, Slack/Telegram bots, and human approval queues.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="pillar-row">
            <div class="pillar-icon" aria-hidden="true">02</div>
            <div>
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-[var(--ink)] mb-1">Cloudflare Sandbox: Isolated Linux Containers</h3>
              <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)] text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Each approved task starts a dedicated Sandbox container with Git, Node.js, Python, and pinned coding CLIs. The container clones the repository, executes the task, produces a unified diff, and terminates.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="pillar-row highlight">
            <div class="pillar-icon accent" aria-hidden="true">03</div>
            <div>
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-[var(--navy)] mb-1">Egress Credential Interception</h3>
              <p class="text-[var(--ink)] text-sm leading-relaxed">
                <strong>Supported provider-backed harnesses use dummy keys.</strong> The Worker authorizes provider and GitHub requests at egress. This is not a universal isolation guarantee: Antigravity OAuth tokens live in a container profile, and live verification remains tracked in the <a href="/docs/readiness/">readiness docs</a>.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="pillar-row">
            <div class="pillar-icon" aria-hidden="true">04</div>
            <div>
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-[var(--ink)] mb-1">Durable Objects &amp; SQLite: State &amp; Streaming</h3>
              <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)] text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Durable Objects provide atomic coordination for human approval state, WebSocket hibernation for streaming container terminal logs, and embedded SQLite for run history.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="pillar-row">
            <div class="pillar-icon" aria-hidden="true">05</div>
            <div>
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-[var(--ink)] mb-1">Cloudflare Access: Zero Trust Authentication</h3>
              <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)] text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Front your dashboard and API endpoints with your team's SSO, email one-time pins, or hardware security keys without running a separate auth service.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 06: COMPARISON -->
    <section class="story-section" aria-labelledby="section-devin">
      <div class="section-label">
        <span class="micro">06 / Comparison</span>
      </div>
      <div class="prose-body">
        <h2 id="section-devin" class="text-[var(--ink)]">Factual comparison: Devin Cloud vs. Shiba</h2>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
          <a href="https://devin.ai" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-[var(--navy)] underline font-bold">Devin Cloud</a> is an impressive hosted service for teams who want zero setup. Shiba is built for teams who want to run in their own cloud account under open-source governance.
        </p>

        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-6 font-mono text-xs">
          <div class="p-5 bg-[var(--card)] border border-[var(--line-2)] shadow-[2px_2px_0_var(--paper-shadow)]">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-[var(--ink)] text-sm mb-3">Devin Cloud</h3>
            <ul class="text-[var(--ink-2)] space-y-2 list-disc pl-4">
              <li><strong>Hosting:</strong> Multi-tenant managed cloud infrastructure hosted by Cognition.</li>
              <li><strong>Model routing:</strong> Fixed, vendor-selected model pipeline.</li>
              <li><strong>Source:</strong> Proprietary closed-source SaaS.</li>
              <li><strong>Execution gate:</strong> Autonomous agent flow with in-run human messaging.</li>
              <li><strong>Pricing:</strong> Flat per-seat monthly subscription plans.</li>
              <li><strong>Audience:</strong> Teams wanting managed infrastructure without self-hosting responsibilities.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="p-5 bg-[var(--card)] border border-[var(--navy)] shadow-[2px_2px_0_var(--paper-shadow)]">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-[var(--navy)] text-sm mb-3">Shiba (Cloudflare Native)</h3>
            <ul class="text-[var(--ink)] space-y-2 list-disc pl-4">
              <li><strong>Hosting:</strong> Deployed in your own Cloudflare account (Workers + Sandbox).</li>
              <li><strong>Model routing:</strong> Pluggable harnesses (OpenCode, Claude Code, Codex, Devin) and custom models.</li>
              <li><strong>Source:</strong> AGPL-3.0-only inspectable open-source repository.</li>
              <li><strong>Execution gate:</strong> Approval card required before any sandbox container boots.</li>
              <li><strong>Pricing:</strong> Pay-as-you-go Cloudflare compute + BYOK model token costs at market rates.</li>
              <li><strong>Audience:</strong> Developers and teams requiring data residency, custom routing, and approval control.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p class="text-xs text-[var(--ink-2)] mt-4">
          For a detailed technical evaluation guide, read our <a href="/open-source-devin-alternative/" class="text-[var(--navy)] underline font-bold">Open Source Devin Alternative Guide</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 07: REACH & INTERFACES -->
    <section class="story-section" aria-labelledby="section-roadmap">
      <div class="section-label">
        <span class="micro">07 / Reach</span>
      </div>
      <div class="prose-body">
        <h2 id="section-roadmap" class="text-[var(--ink)]">Trigger tasks from where your team works</h2>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
          An autonomous agent should integrate into existing developer workflows rather than forcing everyone into a new portal.
        </p>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-6 font-mono text-xs">
          <div class="p-5 bg-[var(--card)] border border-[var(--line-2)] shadow-[2px_2px_0_var(--paper-shadow)]">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-[var(--ink)] text-sm mb-2">Implemented &amp; Tested Locally</h3>
            <ul class="text-[var(--ink-2)] space-y-1.5 list-disc pl-4">
              <li>Web Dashboard (real-time diffs, terminal streaming)</li>
              <li>CLI trigger commands &amp; local runner pairing</li>
              <li>Cron schedule automations (5-minute floor)</li>
              <li>GitHub webhook triggers (issue comments, PR labels)</li>
              <li>Slack bot (@shiba mentions and /shiba commands)</li>
              <li>Telegram and Discord bot triggers</li>
              <li>MCP server gateway (read-only state, queue tasks; cannot approve)</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="p-5 bg-[var(--card)] border border-[var(--navy)] shadow-[2px_2px_0_var(--paper-shadow)]">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-[var(--navy)] text-sm mb-2">Cloud Verification In Progress</h3>
            <ul class="text-[var(--ink)] space-y-1.5 list-disc pl-4">
              <li>End-to-end cloud inference against AI Gateway live endpoints</li>
              <li>Multi-turn Slack approvals across live team channels</li>
              <li>Container peak memory limits under heavy compilation workloads</li>
              <li>Long-running task recovery across WebSocket hibernation cycles</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)] text-sm">
          We maintain full transparency on verified features versus in-development milestones. Track real-time progress on <a href="/docs/readiness/" class="text-[var(--navy)] underline font-bold">Product Readiness</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- 08: RUN IT LOCALLY -->
    <section class="story-section" aria-labelledby="section-deploy">
      <div class="section-label">
        <span class="micro">08 / Quickstart</span>
      </div>
      <div class="prose-body">
        <h2 id="section-deploy" class="text-[var(--ink)]">Get started with Shiba</h2>
        <p class="text-[var(--ink-2)]">
          Prerequisites: Node.js 22.13+, pnpm 10+, and a running Docker daemon for container testing.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-[var(--card)] border border-[var(--line-2)] p-5 font-mono text-xs text-[var(--ink)] my-6 space-y-4 shadow-[2px_2px_0_var(--paper-shadow)] max-w-full overflow-x-auto">
          <div>
            <span class="text-[var(--ink-2)]"># 1. Clone the repository</span><br/>
            <span class="text-[var(--navy)] font-bold">git clone</span> https://github.com/princepal9120/shiba.git<br/>
            <span class="text-[var(--navy)] font-bold">cd</span> shiba &amp;&amp; <span class="text-[var(--navy)] font-bold">pnpm install</span>
          </div>
          <div>
            <span class="text-[var(--ink-2)]"># 2. Configure local environment variables</span><br/>
            <span class="text-[var(--navy)] font-bold">cp</span> apps/backend/.dev.vars.example apps/backend/.dev.vars
          </div>
          <div>
            <span class="text-[var(--ink-2)]"># 3. Run the local development server</span><br/>
            <span class="text-[var(--navy)] font-bold">pnpm dev</span>
          </div>
          <div>
            <span class="text-[var(--ink-2)]"># 4. Verify code quality, tests, and build</span><br/>
            <span class="text-[var(--navy)] font-bold">pnpm typecheck &amp;&amp; pnpm lint &amp;&amp; pnpm test &amp;&amp; pnpm build</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p class="text-xs text-[var(--ink-2)] font-mono">
          For Slack bot setup, GitHub App configuration, and Cloudflare Access policies, see the <a href="/docs/getting-started/" class="text-[var(--navy)] underline font-bold">Quickstart Guide</a>. Source code published under <a href="https://github.com/princepal9120/shiba/blob/main/LICENSE" class="text-[var(--navy)] underline font-bold">AGPL-3.0-only</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- CLOSING CTA -->
    <section class="closing-section">
      <div class="inline-block px-3 py-1.5 bg-[var(--card)] border border-[var(--line-2)] font-mono text-[var(--navy)] text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.14em] mb-4 shadow-[2px_2px_0_var(--paper-shadow)]">
        OWN YOUR CODING AGENT
      </div>
      <h2 class="text-3xl sm:text-5xl font-bold text-[var(--ink)] tracking-tight mb-4">
        Run your coding coworker on your own terms.
      </h2>
      <p class="lead mb-8 text-[var(--ink-2)] mx-auto max-w-2xl">
        Shiba gives you Devin-level autonomy with transparent model routing, strict credential isolation, and approval gates before any code runs. Join our open-source community to build, test, and shape the roadmap.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-actions justify-center">
        <a class="button" href="/waitlist/">
          <span>Join the Waitlist &amp; Community →</span>
        </a>
        <a class="button button-outline" href="/open-source-devin-alternative/">
          <span>Devin Alternative Guide</span>
        </a>
        <a class="button button-outline" href="https://github.com/princepal9120/shiba" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
          <span>Star on GitHub ↗</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</MarketingPage>

<style>
